  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warming trend through the rest of the week.\n\n-  Wildfire smoke will continue to impact the region until \n   further notice.\n\n-  Ridge of high pressure flattens heading into the weekend. Near-\n   critical to critical fire weather expected due to hot, dry and\n   breezy conditions.\n\nA building upper-level ridge will maintain hot, dry, and stable \nconditions across western Montana and north-central Idaho. \nTemperatures will warm significantly Friday and Saturday, with \nlower elevations reaching the 90s and localized spots exceeding \n100 degrees. As the ridge flattens into zonal flow this weekend, \nafternoon surface winds will increase. Hot temperatures, low \nrelative humidity, gusty winds, and critically dry fuels will \nmaintain near-critical to critical fire weather conditions, \npeaking Friday into Saturday. The main focus for the most critical\nconditions is expected across southwest and west-central Montana\nalong with the Palouse and Hells Canyon region of north- central \nIdaho with Fire Weather Watches issued. \n\nWildfire smoke remains a key forecast challenge for air quality \nand visibility. Strong radiational cooling under smoky skies will \nyield sharp nocturnal valley inversions each morning, trapping \nsurface particulates. While daytime boundary layer mixing will \ntemporarily disperse valley smoke each afternoon, increased \nventilation will also invigorate fire activity and generate \nthicker plumes aloft. High-resolution smoke models project surface\nsmoke peaking early this afternoon before modestly improving by \nearly evening.\n\nMonsoonal moisture tracking along the ridge periphery introduces \na slight chance for isolated high-based convection Friday \nafternoon through the weekend, primarily across southwest Montana.\nAn anomalously dry sub-cloud layer will pose a risk for dry \nlightning ignitions and erratic microburst gusts of 40 to 55 mph.\n\nForecast confidence is low to moderate but trending upward \nregarding moisture returning mid/late next week. At this point, \nthe moisture doesn't appear deep or organized enough for \nwidespread rainfall. A substantial synoptic shift such as a deep \nPacific Northwest trough will be required to suppress fire \nactivity and clear smoke. Ensemble guidance shows a 20 to 30% \nchance of this trough arriving by mid to late next week.",
